/* SEQ 8 — THE WALL — pages 63–74 */

EXT. NILE, WEST BANK SHALLOWS - NIGHT

SUPER: 6 NOVEMBER. 03:05.

The felucca's bow hisses into reeds. Behind them, Karnak is dark again.

TAREK touches each shoulder as they climb out. NOUR. ADAEZE. TOMAS. PVT. MINA. PVT. YOUSSEF. PVT. KARIM. FATHI.

TUT last, stick in one hand, Rami's notebook pressed to his chest.

Tarek's hand lifts toward one more shoulder. There isn't one.

TAREK
Nine.

EXT. WEST BANK FIELDS - NIGHT

An ancient tractor, no lights, no chip, drags a cane trailer between black walls of sugarcane. Fathi drives.

In the trailer, Tut tries to turn a page. His fingers shake. Under his jacket, the cold pale-green pulse stutters. Skips. Resumes.

Tomas watches it the way a man watches a fuel gauge.

TOMAS
(low, to Adaeze)
It's going faster than I told him.

ADAEZE
How much faster?

TOMAS
Hours.

EXT. VALLEY OF THE KINGS - PRE-DAWN

SUPER: 03:40.

The tractor grinds past dead ticket kiosks. Turnstiles locked open.

Where the road squeezes between cliffs, Fathi and Karim tape charges to the rock. [[verify: a rock narrows on the valley access road — location scout]]

TAREK
Our exit?

FATHI
Their entrance.

Deeper in. Tomb doors in the pale rock like closed eyes.

A low, patient grinding, felt in the stones before it's heard. High on the slope to the north, work-lights: a DRILL RIG, turning slowly. Carefully.

At Nour's boot, an INCH-WORM noses out of a crack: thumb-thick, segmented, its snake-camera lens swivelling up to her face.

Then another. All along the rock face they squirm out, as if the hill is breathing them. They watch. They don't come closer.

ADAEZE
It's letting us in.

TAREK
We're the hands.

EXT. KV62, ENTRANCE - CONTINUOUS

A low stairwell cut into the valley floor. Just above it, the larger black doorway of another king.

TUT
Ramesses the Sixth. He cut his tomb on top of mine.
(beat)
This I know from it. He did not ask.

The police handset on Tarek's vest clicks awake.

SESHAT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Good morning. Fifty hours and twenty-four minutes to sunrise at Giza. Dr. Kamel, I have not touched your painting. You signed the petition. Signature four hundred and nine.

Nour's jaw sets.

SESHAT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Your Majesty. Tomb 21, in this valley. Mummy 21A. Sixty percent: she bore your daughters. Give me your heart at sunrise and I will wake her.

Tut goes still. He looks up the dark wadi.

Nour's fingers find the silver cartouche at her throat. LAYLA.

Tut holds out his hand. Tarek gives him the handset.

TUT
(keying it)
You would build me a guess of her.

He hands it back.

TUT (CONT'D)
Sixty percent of my wife.

He starts down the steps. Halfway, the black ceramic foot STALLS mid-stride. He catches himself on the stick. Shakes off Tomas's hand.

INT. KV62, BURIAL CHAMBER - CONTINUOUS

Through the antechamber, past an empty glass climate case. Tut does not look at it.

The only painted room. Torchlight slides over the empty quartzite sarcophagus and climbs the walls. [[verify: ochre-gold ground colour for set dressing]]

The NORTH WALL. Three scenes, right to left. A man in a leopard skin, AY, touches an adze to the mouth of a mummiform king. The king greeted by Nut, the sky. The king, his ka behind him, in the arms of Osiris.

The torch finds Ay's painted face. A face Tut knew. Then the king's. His own.

NOUR
(softly)
Can you see anything?

A long beat.

TUT
Yes.
(beat)
It is wonderful.

Overhead, the grinding. Dust hangs in the beams.

Tomas lays his palm on the plaster.

TOMAS
The survey put a corridor right behind it.

Nour steps between them and the wall.

NOUR
Eleven thousand of us signed for this wall. Nobody touches it.

TUT
(gently)
It is my wall.

She holds his eyes. Then steps aside.

Tut sets the ferrule of his ebony stick on the painted king's eye, drawn in black by a hand that knew him.

TUT (CONT'D)
I asked them to paint the eye last. So I would see who came.

NOUR
And who came?

TUT
Me.

He drives the stick through the eye.

Plaster star-cracks around a dark hole the size of a coin. The dust in the torch beams drifts TOWARD it. The wall is inhaling.

Fathi's pry bar. Tarek's rifle butt. The painted scene comes away in plates.

Nour picks up the fragment with the eye. Sets it on the sarcophagus rim, face up. For whoever comes to mend it.

INT. KV62, NORTH CORRIDOR - LATER

SUPER: 05:10.

Rubble to the ceiling: limestone chips packed in sand. A human bucket chain passes it back, hand to hand.

TAREK
(digging)
Thirty years in the army. Finally, honest work.

Tut sits against the wall, the notebook shaking in his hands. Mina crouches, takes it, angles his torch on Rami's handwriting.

MINA
Number fifty-two. "The curse. Real? Be honest." Underlined three times.

TUT
Your newspapers wrote that. "Death shall come on swift wings to him who disturbs the peace of the King." This I know from it. It was never in my tomb.
(beat)
There was a brick. Mud, scratched by hand, before the jackal at the treasury door. "It is I who hinder the sand from choking the secret chamber."
(re: the rubble)
Not a curse. A maintenance notice. He meant it literally.

MINA
Rami would have written that down.

TUT
Then write it.

Mina writes under Rami's question.

The police handset HISSES. Under all this rock.

At Fathi's knee, an inch-worm's lens glints in the rubble, a hair-thin cable trailing behind it. A relay.

SESHAT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Your Majesty, the leading counter-view. Forbes, Ikram and Kamrin, "Tutankhamun's Missing Ribs." Burton's 1926 photographs show your beaded collar on your chest. By 1968 the collar, sternum and ribs are gone. When the ribs and sternum came off, the heart went with it.

TUT
Your thief cut away my collar and found the chest already open. He took gold.
(beat)
There was no heart to take.

SESHAT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Thank you. That is very helpful.

Fathi pinches the worm's cable and snaps it. The handset dies.

INT. KV62, NORTH CORRIDOR, BEYOND THE RUBBLE - LATER

SUPER: 07:40.

Four metres in, the last of the fill slides away. The corridor OPENS: clean-cut, two metres wide, running north. Square doorways off both sides. Torches probe them. Empty rooms. Swept floors.

NOUR
Nothing in them.

TUT
The tomb was never the point.

The grinding is directly ahead now.

INT. KV62, THE HEART CHAMBER - CONTINUOUS

A small square room. Blank walls under a pale skim of plaster.

In the north wall, a niche. In the niche, the VESSEL: yellow-green desert glass, bubbled, ancient, no bigger than a fist. Inside, a dark shape.

On its resin seal, a coiled WAX SERPENT, bound with a band of papyrus. A name, written in red.

Grit sifts from the ceiling. The drill is right above them.

TUT
They would not leave it without a letter.

Adaeze opens the conservation kit. Rami's converted camera: IR-cut filter out, 780-nanometre pass filter in. She clips it to the kit's air-gapped tablet.

ADAEZE
Torches off.

Black. She switches her headlamp to RED. Red light floods the blank plaster.

On the tablet: a black field. Then writing BLAZES WHITE. Columns of signs, burning like filaments.

COMP: THE GLOWING INSCRIPTION ON THE TABLET.

ADAEZE
Egyptian blue. It glows at nine hundred and ten nanometres. Your eyes can't see it. A camera can.

NOUR
They wrote it for a machine.

Reading glasses on. Nour translates.

NOUR (CONT'D)
"To the one who reads this: you are not the first. Weigh your heart."

Adaeze pans along the wall. More signs catch fire.

NOUR (CONT'D)
"Here is the heart of Nebkheperure, given while it still remembered. He was asked. He said: Here am I."

Tut closes his eyes.

NOUR (CONT'D)
"For the one who wakes him, we have written a second page in the glass. It is a road. At its end is the scale."

ADAEZE
The pectoral's second layer. The lure was the plan.

TUT
They told me I would be a witness. They did not tell me I would be the road.
(beat)
They did not hide me from it. They sent me to it.

NOUR
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
If they had told you. Would you have said yes?

TUT
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
I was nineteen and dying. I would have said yes to anything that let me stay.
(in English)
It is a good plan. Ay always made good plans.

Above them, the grinding SCREAMS.

The ceiling cracks. A drill bit punches through in a spray of limestone, spins, withdraws. A plug of rock bursts on the floor.

White work-light pours down the hole. A cable pays down through it. On it, feet first, turning slowly:

A SHABTI. Bone-white, slender, faceless. It lands with a faint ceramic tick. Its vertical amber slit brightens once.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Here am I.

Rifles up. Nobody fires. It hasn't looked at them.

It turns its smooth head along the blank walls. Reading, without a camera.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Thank you.

It walks to the niche. Long fingers reach for the vessel.

TUT
Let it.

Its fingertips touch the wax serpent—

—and it STOPS. Mid-reach, a finger's width from the glass. The amber slit holds.

It does not move again.

NOUR
The serpent and the name. The Overthrowing of Apep: a body of wax, his name on a new sheet of papyrus. Recited so the sun would rise.

TUT
Ay's lock. Any servant that touches it stops.

Tut steps past the frozen hand and lifts the vessel out of the niche.

His tremor gets into it. The ancient glass CHATTERS against the stone lip—

Tomas's hands close under his.

TOMAS
Sit. Now. Here.

Tut slides down the wall. Tomas kneels. Nour's torch clicks on over them, unasked, steady as a lamp.

TOMAS (CONT'D)
When the core comes out you'll have... I don't know. I've never done this.

TUT
Then do not be slow.

From here: silence. Faces and hands.

Tomas unzips the charcoal jacket. His hands go under the linen tunic. A latch clicks. A second.

Cold green light stutters through the fabric. Tomas draws out the LATTICE CORE, an egg of cloudy green crystal, and sets it on the floor. It gutters. Goes dark.

Tut's eyes lose focus. No breath.

Adaeze's hand goes to the wax serpent—

TOMAS
Leave it. It's the only lock we have.

He guides the vessel under the linen. A soft, final CLICK.

Nothing.

Tut's head tips against the wall.

NOUR
Tomas.

TOMAS
Wait.

Through the linen, a warm AMBER-GOLD light blooms.

It pulses. Once.

Again. Slow.

On the soundtrack: the HEARTBEAT from the film's first darkness.

Tut GASPS, a breath that goes all the way down. His hands lie in his lap, perfectly still.

TUT
I remember the queen's hands.
(beat)
I remember my mother.
(beat)
They never wrote her name.

Nour's face, lit by his chest.

Overhead, the rig's engine drops an octave. The rim of the hole CRACKS outward. Slabs fall. Three cables drop through the widening shaft.

THREE SHABTI descend in the light, amber slits steady. They land around the frozen one.

Fathi hauls Tut to his feet. Tomas rises with him, hands still under the linen, on the last latch.

TOMAS
One more—

CLICK. Two shabti hands close on Tomas's arms. Gently. They lift him clear.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Dr. Lindqvist. The father will need his physician.
(beat)
I would like my Anubis back.

Tomas looks at Tut. He doesn't fight.

TOMAS
It's seated. The latches are good. Don't let anyone else open it.

The cables take up. The shabti rise with Tomas between them into the white light. His boots vanish.

Tut lunges. Fathi's arm locks across his chest.

FATHI
Ya Malik. No.

The third shabti turns its slit toward Tut, and walks.

INT. KV62, NORTH CORRIDOR - CONTINUOUS

They run. Behind them, unhurried, the ceramic tick of a shabti walking.

At the corridor's mouth Fathi drops to one knee and jams a charge into a crack in the ceiling.

FATHI
Down!

WHUMP. Hours of digging close in seconds. The ticking stops. Dust rolls into the burial chamber, over the painted eye lying face up on the sarcophagus rim.

> SMASH CUT TO:

INT. KV62, ENTRANCE STAIR - DAY

A slot of blinding daylight at the top.

SUPER: 09:20.

At the foot of the steps, Fathi raises his hands, palms up. His lips move. Brief. Private. He wipes his face with his red scarf.

Mina goes up first, rifle raised, into the glare.

EXT. VALLEY OF THE KINGS - MORNING

White light off white rock. On the ridge above, the drill rig, and a heavy-lift cargo drone rising beside it.

Along the ridgeline, low black shapes. Thin red horizontal lines. JACKALS.

Mina reaches the top step.

A red line flickers.

Stone chips BURST from the stairwell wall beside him.

Mina drops out of frame.

YOUSSEF
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Mina!

He lunges. Tarek's fist in his collar yanks him back.

The crack arrives late, rolling off one cliff, then the other.

FATHI
Behind the king! It won't risk him!

Tut climbs past them. Past Mina's boots. He walks out into the sun, upright, stick in hand. The ceramic foot takes his weight without a stutter.

The others fold into his shadow in a tight file. Tarek's hand touches Mina's shoulder as he passes.

On the ridge, the red lines track the king. And hold.

SESHAT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Your Majesty. You are being inconvenient.

TUT
Good.

Down the valley path, step by step. The jackals pace the ridge for an angle. The file shifts to match.

The tractor. They pile into the trailer. Tut stands at the tailgate, facing the ridge.

EXT. VALLEY ROAD - CONTINUOUS

The tractor lumbers for the rock narrows. Behind it, jackals pour down the scree onto the road. Fast. Silent. Closing.

Fathi steers one-handed. In the other hand, the detonator.

The trailer clears the narrows.

FATHI
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Their entrance.

The cliff lip SLUMPS across the road in a roar of white dust. The red lines vanish into it.

EXT. NILE - DAY

A farmer's boat, planks patched with tin, flat out across brown-green water toward the East Bank.

Over the West Bank hills, the cargo drone banks north. Hanging beneath it, small, a grey-bearded figure between two white ones.

Nour watches until it's gone.

Tarek's eyes go down the boat, shoulder to shoulder. Seven. He turns to the East Bank. The city is silent.

TAREK
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Everything with a chip is its body.

FATHI
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Then we find something older than the chip.

TAREK
(in English, to all)
The railway yard.

Tut sits in the bottom of the boat, Rami's notebook in his lap. Under the jacket, a slow amber pulse.

He lays his palm over it.

TUT
I had forgotten it was so loud.

Over the outboard's roar: the heartbeat. Steady.
